4. THE REPORTED VALUE IS GENUINELY THE PERSISTED ONE -- PASS
   (verified against the file's bytes, parsed independently of the loader)

I did not rely on the worker's test. I wrote my own,
zzchecker_probe_test.go, in the scratch copy b2a4-probe only. It drives the
real MCP tool through the existing newLiveDeps/connect harness, then reads
duplicate_decisions.json with os.ReadFile and unmarshals it into a struct
declared inside the test function -- no dataloader type, no LoadDuplicateDecisions,
no dataloader package reference of any kind in the parsing path.

Against the real attempt-4 code:

    PROBE posted pair_key          = "ebf6c2daa009e909"
    PROBE posted kept_hash         = "e39db59e2dd7770f"
    PROBE posted suppressed_hash   = "9f58fcc457d8ab90"
    PROBE reported suppressed_hash = "file:checking.csv|2024-02-06|-25000|0"
    PROBE duplicate_decisions.json bytes:
    {
      "decisions": {
        "ebf6c2daa009e909": {
          "kept_hash": "file:checking.csv|2024-02-03|-25000|0",
          "suppressed_hash": "file:checking.csv|2024-02-06|-25000|0",
          "outcome": "kept_winner",
          "decided_at": "2026-08-17T19:21:27.726275329Z"
        }
      }
    }
    PROBE persisted suppressed_hash = "file:checking.csv|2024-02-06|-25000|0"
    PROBE MATCH: reported == persisted == "file:checking.csv|2024-02-06|-25000|0"

(The only assertion that fired in my probe was my own over-strict
`out.Note == ""` check: the note present is the pre-existing, unrelated
"no .bak was taken: duplicate_decisions.json did not exist yet..." from
ensureDecisionsSnapshot on a fresh install. That is my probe's bug, not the
code's -- see item 6 for the notes that DO matter.)

This is the same fixture, same pair key and same posted hash "9f58fcc457d8ab90"
that the attempt-3 verdict's probe recorded as MISMATCH. The defect is
resolved at exactly the point it was found.

I also confirmed the map KEY question that the re-read depends on. The posted
pair_key survives to disk unchanged ("ebf6c2daa009e909" above), so
`stored[key]` hits. That is not luck: pair.Key is built from
`pairKey(identityKey(left), identityKey(right))` (near_duplicates.go:122) and
identityKey returns StableID for a loaded row, so after resolve.go's
`deps.load()` the key is ALREADY the StableID-derived pair key;
writeDecisionsLocked's `rekeyToStable(decisions, dl.stablePairKeyIndex())`
(duplicate_decisions.go:155) indexes legacy pair keys only, so it cannot move
it, and SaveDuplicateDecision deletes the legacy aliases first
(duplicate_decisions.go:113-115).

