OBSERVATIONS -- neither blocks acceptance; recorded for the lead
=====================================================================

O1. The comment at resolve.go:177-182 is muddled and should not be read as a
    statement of fact: "SaveDuplicateDecision does not rekey the map KEY on its
    own (that pass lives in writeDecisionsLocked, which SaveDuplicateDecision
    also calls)" -- the parenthetical concedes what the main clause denies, and
    writeDecisionsLocked DOES rekey map keys (duplicate_decisions.go:155). The
    CONCLUSION it draws is nonetheless correct, and I verified it two ways
    (item 4): the posted pair_key is already a StableID-derived key by the time
    it is used, so rekeyToStable cannot move it, and the probe shows the entry
    persisted under the posted key. Comment wording only.

O2. In the two fallback branches the field carries the posted value while the
    schema description says "as it is persisted". The `note` in the same output
    object states this explicitly and in plain words, and this fallback is the
    behavior the authorization for attempt 4 specifies, so I do not treat the
    description as false. If the lead wants belt-and-braces, the description
    could add "unless note says otherwise".

O3. Still open from attempt 3, unchanged and outside this scope:
    internal/services/mcpsvc/admin/undo.go:57 pre-checks `decisions[key]`
    without consulting dl.legacyPairKeysFor(key), so undo_resolve can report
    "has no decision recorded" for a pre-StableID decision that
    ClearDuplicateDecision would in fact have cleared. It fails loudly rather
    than silently and predates attempt 3.

